.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH shellagotchi 1  "shellagotchi " 
.SH NAME
shellagotchi
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBshellagotchi\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
shellagotchi\-feed(1)
Report a command\*(Aqs exit status to the daemon (used by the shell hook)
.TP
shellagotchi\-daemon(1)
Run the shellagotchi daemon (the process the shell hook and CLI subcommands talk to over a Unix socket)
.TP
shellagotchi\-prompt(1)
Print a rendered prompt segment, reading ONLY the prompt cache file the daemon maintains (never the IPC socket). This makes it safe and fast enough to embed directly in a shell `PS1`
.TP
shellagotchi\-status(1)
Show the pet\*(Aqs current status as a bordered ASCII card, fetched live from the daemon over IPC
.TP
shellagotchi\-show(1)
Alias for `status` (the plan treats `show` and `status` as the same command)
.TP
shellagotchi\-watch(1)
Launch a live, interactively\-updating terminal UI showing the pet\*(Aqs sprite, mood, and stat gauges, polling the daemon in the background. Keybinds: q=quit, c=clean, p=pet, r=refresh
.TP
shellagotchi\-init(1)
Print the shell integration snippet for `shell` to stdout, meant to be evaluated/sourced directly into an rc file, e.g. `eval "$(shellagotchi init bash)"` in `~/.bashrc`. Prints *exactly* the snippet with no extra logging, since the caller feeds stdout straight into `eval`/`source`
.TP
shellagotchi\-doctor(1)
Run diagnostic checks (config, socket, daemon, rc\-file hooks) and print a human\-readable report. Exits non\-zero if any check fails
.TP
shellagotchi\-install(1)
Write the systemd user unit file and, if a systemd user session is available, enable + start the daemon via it
.TP
shellagotchi\-hatch(1)
Replace a dead pet with a brand\-new newborn one. Only works while the current pet is actually dead (health reached 0); the old pet\*(Aqs final state is archived to the graveyard log before the new one is created
.TP
shellagotchi\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
